  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 101 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nScattered higher based showers and storms dropping southeast from\nKS Thursday morning will spread across portions of northeast OK\nand northwest AR. Broken mid and high level clouds for much of the\nday across the region should begin to scatter out from west to\neast during the afternoon, allowing temperatures to quickly\nrebound. With forecast highs in the mid to upper 90s and the high\ndewpoints in place in the vicinity of the stalled front, forecast\nheat indices are again expected to reach advisory levels over much\nof eastern OK and west-central AR. A few locations could get close\nto warning levels, but will let the next shift iron those details\nout especially given uncertainties with cloud cover. Expect more\nscattered showers and storms in the vicinity of the stalled front\nover far eastern OK/western AR, which will be oriented more south\nto north by this time as the ridge aloft to our west holds strong\nand may even strengthen a bit with some subtle height rises \nindicated in the data.\n\nA shortwave trough/PV max noted in RAP analysis and WV imagery\nover CA and the Great Basin is expected to top the ridge and then\ndrop southeast over the Plains Thursday night through Friday\nnight, strengthening as it heads equatorward. The latest CAMs\nshow a large MCS organizing with the system over the central\nPlains Thursday night, with some of this activity possibly\ndropping down into northeast OK by Friday morning. The surface\ncool front is expected to push south into the region Friday\nafternoon, with at least a few storms likely to focus along the\nboundary into the evening as it pushes south. With the front not\narriving until later in the day, forecast highs are again expected\nto climb to around triple digits across eastern OK and west-\ncentral AR. Another round of heat headlines will probably be \nneeded for Friday as well, but that should be it for this stretch.\n\nRelatively cooler and drier air will filter into the region behind\nthe front for the weekend. Below average temps for the first of\nAugust and lower humidity will make for ideal conditions for\noutdoor activities this weekend. Surface ridging is still in place\ninto Monday before south winds and higher dewpoints make a return\nby midweek.\n\nLacy",
